QEMU 11.1.1 linux-user: a new thread's host signal mask is its guest mask.

paslang's arm64 tests run under QEMU's user mode, built from the signed
11.1.1 release with this patch (scripts/build_qemu.py). A thread that
spins in guest code never took a signal again, and paslang's collector,
which stops such a thread with SIGURG (P293, as Go's asynchronous
preemption), waited for it for ever (paslang P302, found on
testdata/whilerun under the collector's stress: 6 hangs in 600 runs,
at once under load):

- host_signal_handler() (linux-user/signal.c) leaves the interrupted
  context with every host signal blocked but SIGSEGV and SIGBUS, until
  process_pending_signals() sets the host mask back to the guest's;
- a guest clone() that runs in that window (a signal came just before
  the thread's svc, and do_syscall() runs before the signal is
  processed) saves that all-blocked mask in do_fork() and clone_func()
  gives it to the new thread;
- the new thread's TaskState has no signal pending, so its
  process_pending_signals() never sets its mask, and every signal sent
  to it stays pending in the host (/proc's SigBlk fffffffe7ffbfabf,
  SigPnd SIGURG, signal_pending 0 and an empty guest mask, the thread in
  translated code).

The new thread now takes the guest mask it inherited (ts->signal_mask,
copied from the parent's) without SIGSEGV and SIGBUS, as
process_pending_signals() sets it. With it, whilerun ran 1000 times
beside 16 busy loops without a hang, where it hung within its first
three runs before. QEMU's master branch has the same code.

--- a/linux-user/syscall.c
+++ b/linux-user/syscall.c
@@ -6948,8 +6948,22 @@
     if (info->parent_tidptr)
         put_user_u32(info->tid, info->parent_tidptr);
     qemu_guest_random_seed_thread_part2(cpu->random_seed);
-    /* Enable signals.  */
-    sigprocmask(SIG_SETMASK, &info->sigmask, NULL);
+    /*
+     * Enable signals: the host mask mirrors the guest's (ts->signal_mask,
+     * copied from the parent's), as process_pending_signals() leaves it,
+     * not the parent's host mask of the moment, which is all-blocked from
+     * host_signal_handler() until the parent's process_pending_signals().
+     * A thread created in that window kept every host signal but SIGSEGV
+     * and SIGBUS blocked, with no signal pending to make it set its mask,
+     * and never took a signal again.
+     */
+    {
+        sigset_t set = ts->signal_mask;
+
+        sigdelset(&set, SIGSEGV);
+        sigdelset(&set, SIGBUS);
+        sigprocmask(SIG_SETMASK, &set, NULL);
+    }
     /* Signal to the parent that we're ready.  */
     pthread_mutex_lock(&info->mutex);
     pthread_cond_broadcast(&info->cond);
